 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+Eviction uses 2Q. A newly admitted sheet enters `A1in`, a FIFO queue for first-use entries. A sheet accessed again while still resident promotes to `Am`, a recency-ordered queue for reused entries; a hit within `Am` moves the entry to its most-recent position. Both queues are bounded by a fraction of the 12 GiB budget. When admitting a completed sheet would exceed the budget, eviction takes from `A1in` first and only draws from `Am` once `A1in` is empty, so a long sequential screening displaces its own one-off sheets before it can displace another editor's comparison set. This is the two-queue form of 2Q — there is no separate ghost queue tracking recently evicted first-use keys. The change is confined to this replacement policy: the external interface, queue concurrency, and disk budget are unchanged.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|--------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (`A1in`/`Am` split, two queues, no ghost queue) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | See `decisions/eviction-policy.md`. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. What fraction of the 12 GiB budget is allocated to `A1in` vs. `Am`, and is the split fixed or tunable per production?
